  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1103 AM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAnother active late afternoon and evening expected with an almost\nexact same pattern as yesterday. The main difference is that the\nmoisture will be move out of western zones and the focus will be\nthe RGV east. Shear and instability remain in place, so initial\ncells will be capable of producing large hail and damaging winds.\nGood agreement among CAMs that storms will congeal into a bow type\necho as it heads east out of the area. The NBM PoPs seemed too\nhigh over the west and tapered back some. \n\nFor Sun, upper low that will be bringing us the weather today \nwill be lifting NE into UT/CO border region and drier air will \ncontinue a eastward push. Again, models agree that the drier air \nmakes it into the RGV and possibly as far east as the Hudspeth \ncounty line. There will remain shear and decent instability over \nfar eastern zones, so a few at least marginally severe storms are \npossible. Areas to the west will see west to southwest winds and \nshould be able to warm up nicely into the 70s to lower 80s. Monday\nthe models are differing on a westward push of moisture/cooler\nair. The GFS continues to be the odd model out and keeps moisture\neast while others bring it at least through the Continental\nDivide. AIGFS is also looking closer to EC/NAM so will go that way\nwhich mainly affects temps. Slightly cooler then for Mon,\nespecially eastern zones. Will also keep shower/thunderstorm\nchances going out there and again, a few severe are not out of the\nquestion.  \n\nA westerly flow gradually pushes moisture and instability east of\nthe area for Tue/Wed. Not much chance if any for precipitation and\ntemperatures will be the warmest of the forecast period.  \n\nAnother storm system moves into the West Coast for the end of the\nweek with southwest flow tapping some more sub-tropical moisture\nand rain chances return. Could be some good rainfall amounts for\nThu/Fri. Models showing some shear over the area but instability\nisn't as high as recent days. Temperatures will start cooling \ndown with increased cloud cover and moisture.",
